<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Léon Blum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Léon Blum was a French socialist politician and writer, the first socialist and first Jewish prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> government from June 1936 to June 1937 and briefly in 1938, he introduced paid holidays, the 40-hour week and collective bargaining. Tried by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> regime and deported by the Germans, he survived the war and headed a short caretaker government in 1946–1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leon-blum.jpg" alt="Léon Blum" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bulletin municipal de la ville de Toulouse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%A9on_Blum_en_1936.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
